WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
Between 1922 and 1947, lung cancer deaths in

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.880
England and Wales rose about fifteen times.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.280
Smoking had grown, but so had tarred roads,

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.280
car exhaust and factory smoke.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.360
In 1950, Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.920
published the first of their studies on smoking.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.520
So how do we judge an epidemiological study,

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:24.880
and what it achieved?

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.060
Earlier, you measured disease with rates.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.700
Now we judge the studies behind them.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:35.380
An observational study watches what people already do,

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:38.580
such as smoking, and compares their disease rates.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:42.260
In an intervention study, researchers decide who gets

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:43.380
an exposure.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.740
In a randomised controlled trial, chance decides the

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:49.420
groups, which helps balance other differences.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.340
But nobody could ethically make people smoke for

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:55.060
decades, so the smoking evidence had to be

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:56.100
observational.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.340
Their first study was a case-control study: it

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.820
starts with the disease and looks back.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:05.860
They interviewed about seven hundred lung cancer patients

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.780
in twenty London hospitals, each matched with a

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:11.780
control, a patient without lung cancer, of the

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:13.940
same sex and similar age.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.260
Then they compared smoking histories.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:19.860
Nearly every man with lung cancer smoked, and

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.100
heavy smokers were far more common among the

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.260
patients than among the controls.

00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:29.386
To judge any method, use four steps: design,

00:01:29.627 --> 00:01:32.106
strength, limitation, judgement.

00:01:32.427 --> 00:01:34.186
The design is case-control.

00:01:34.587 --> 00:01:37.386
Its strength is speed, and matching made age

00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:39.226
and sex more comparable.

00:01:39.307 --> 00:01:42.186
Its limitation is that smoking was remembered, not

00:01:42.427 --> 00:01:43.866
recorded at the time.

00:01:43.947 --> 00:01:46.266
That opens the door to bias, a flaw

00:01:46.307 --> 00:01:48.586
in the method that pushes results one way,

00:01:48.667 --> 00:01:50.666
which more data can't cancel.

00:01:50.707 --> 00:01:54.106
The judgement: a strong association, but not yet

00:01:54.267 --> 00:01:55.306
proof of cause.

00:01:56.534 --> 00:01:59.653
Critics asked whether a third factor, perhaps something

00:01:59.774 --> 00:02:02.853
inherited, made people both more likely to smoke

00:02:03.094 --> 00:02:05.093
and more likely to get lung cancer.

00:02:05.734 --> 00:02:08.053
A factor linked to both the exposure and

00:02:08.134 --> 00:02:09.973
the disease is a confounder.

00:02:10.934 --> 00:02:13.093
Doll and Hill's next study was a cohort

00:02:13.174 --> 00:02:15.813
study, which follows a group forward in time.

00:02:16.454 --> 00:02:19.573
In 1951, they posted a smoking questionnaire to

00:02:19.734 --> 00:02:20.533
British doctors.

00:02:21.094 --> 00:02:24.693
About forty thousand replied, and year after year,

00:02:24.774 --> 00:02:27.733
the researchers recorded which doctors died, and from

00:02:27.894 --> 00:02:28.213
what.

00:02:29.434 --> 00:02:31.913
Doctors were easy to trace, and their causes

00:02:31.994 --> 00:02:34.073
of death were well recorded.

00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:37.113
By 1954, the lung cancer death rate rose

00:02:37.194 --> 00:02:40.873
steadily with the amount smoked: lowest in non-smokers,

00:02:40.954 --> 00:02:42.873
highest in heavy smokers.

00:02:43.194 --> 00:02:46.713
That climbing pattern is a dose-response relationship.

00:02:46.754 --> 00:02:49.113
And because smoking was recorded before the later

00:02:49.194 --> 00:02:51.673
deaths, the exposure came first.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:54.260
Now the four steps again.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.500
Design: cohort.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:00.580
Strengths: smoking was recorded before the deaths, not

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:04.260
reconstructed afterwards, and tens of thousands of doctors

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:06.260
were followed for fifty years.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:10.660
Limitations: doctors aren't a typical population, most were

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:13.460
men, and smoking was self-reported.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.820
It's still observational, so an unmeasured confounder can't

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:18.500
be fully ruled out.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:21.780
Judgement: the strongest design that was ethical.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.120
So how did an association become a cause?

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.560
Several kinds of evidence pointed the same way.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.760
The link was strong, it showed a dose-response,

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:34.840
and exposure came first.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.160
Studies in other countries agreed.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.360
Carcinogens in smoke damage the DNA of airway

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.600
cells, which gives a mechanism.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:45.880
The fifty-year follow-up found that quitting around thirty

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:48.600
avoided almost all the extra risk of dying

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:49.240
early.

00:03:50.467 --> 00:03:52.386
Now, what did it achieve?

00:03:52.947 --> 00:03:55.586
A benefit isn't the finding itself.

00:03:55.627 --> 00:03:57.906
It's what the finding lets people do.

00:03:58.227 --> 00:04:01.186
Picture a chain: the study identifies a risk

00:04:01.267 --> 00:04:03.706
factor that can be changed, someone makes a

00:04:03.747 --> 00:04:07.106
decision, the decision reaches people, and new data

00:04:07.267 --> 00:04:09.266
check whether disease fell.

00:04:09.307 --> 00:04:12.866
In Australia, the evidence supported warning labels, advertising

00:04:12.947 --> 00:04:16.946
bans, taxes, quit programs and plain packaging.

00:04:17.027 --> 00:04:19.506
Daily smoking fell to around one adult in

00:04:19.587 --> 00:04:22.386
ten, and men's lung cancer rates have fallen

00:04:22.507 --> 00:04:24.146
since the early 1980s.

00:04:25.367 --> 00:04:28.566
The benefit was large, mainly because smoking was

00:04:28.847 --> 00:04:30.806
common and could be changed.

00:04:30.887 --> 00:04:33.446
But the chain was slow: the evidence was

00:04:33.647 --> 00:04:36.966
strong by the mid-1950s, yet the biggest policies

00:04:37.127 --> 00:04:38.486
took decades.

00:04:38.567 --> 00:04:41.526
It's also unequal, because smoking is still more

00:04:41.687 --> 00:04:43.393
common in some groups.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:46.406
And a study asks something of its participants:

00:04:46.727 --> 00:04:48.486
time and privacy.

00:04:48.567 --> 00:04:50.646
The classic mistake is saying a study is

00:04:50.807 --> 00:04:52.886
beneficial the moment it's published.

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:55.740
Two exam questions.

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:56.500
First.

00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:00.340
Researchers found three hundred adults with bladder cancer

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:03.460
and six hundred without, from the same hospitals,

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:05.140
matched for age and sex.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:07.860
Each was asked how often they'd used hair

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:09.780
dye over the past twenty years.

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:12.780
Evaluate the method used in this study.

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:14.420
Pause here to answer.

00:05:24.834 --> 00:05:27.873
It's case-control: it starts with the disease and

00:05:27.954 --> 00:05:28.833
looks back.

00:05:29.074 --> 00:05:32.033
It's quick, and matching reduced differences in age

00:05:32.074 --> 00:05:32.753
and sex.

00:05:33.394 --> 00:05:35.633
But hair dye use was recalled over twenty

00:05:35.794 --> 00:05:39.313
years, and people with cancer may remember differently:

00:05:39.394 --> 00:05:40.433
recall bias.

00:05:40.994 --> 00:05:43.873
So it shows an association, not a cause.

00:05:44.434 --> 00:05:47.393
The classic mistake is thinking matching removes every

00:05:47.554 --> 00:05:48.193
confounder.

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:50.673
It only controls what was matched.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:52.380
Second.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:55.980
Studies in the 1980s and 90s linked low

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:59.980
folate around conception with neural tube defects, where

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:02.380
the early brain or spinal cord does not

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:03.580
close properly.

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:07.900
From 2009, non-organic Australian bread flour had to

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:09.740
contain folic acid.

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:13.820
Afterwards, neural tube defects fell, most in higher-risk

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:14.300
groups.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:16.940
Evaluate the benefit of these studies.

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:18.780
Pause here to answer.

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:31.280
The studies found a risk factor that could

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:32.560
be changed.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:35.440
Fortified flour reached people without them having to

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:38.800
act, which matters, because the neural tube closes

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:41.280
before many people know they're pregnant.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.160
Follow-up data showed the fall, largest in the

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:45.973
highest-risk groups.

00:06:46.027 --> 00:06:48.560
A large benefit, though other changes may have

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:49.600
helped.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:52.560
The classic mistake is saying the studies themselves

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:54.080
prevented the defects.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:57.180
So, for any study.

00:06:57.460 --> 00:06:59.940
To judge the method, give the design, a

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:04.740
strength, a limitation, then decide: association or cause?

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:08.740
To judge the benefit, follow the chain: finding,

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:11.300
decision, action, outcome checked.

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:15.300
Next, you'll practise rates, graphs and study judgements

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:15.860
in exam

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:16.580
questions.

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:19.240
Here are the key notes.

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:21.400
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:22.280
to copy them.
